WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.195
Lesson 2.2.

00:00:02.195 --> 00:00:05.965
AMM mathematics (x · y = k).

00:00:05.965 --> 00:00:08.599
By the end, you'll be able to calculate what

00:00:08.599 --> 00:00:11.700
a swap actually pays out in a constant-product pool,

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:14.451
and explain why bigger trades get worse prices.

00:00:15.751 --> 00:00:17.041
Here's the plan.

00:00:17.041 --> 00:00:18.909
First, the one rule that prices

00:00:18.909 --> 00:00:21.486
every trade in a constant-product pool.

00:00:21.486 --> 00:00:24.046
Second, a real worked example, with the

00:00:24.046 --> 00:00:26.266
exact numbers a swap pays out.

00:00:26.266 --> 00:00:28.653
And third, the checklist you run before any

00:00:28.653 --> 00:00:30.661
swap that touches one of these pools.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:34.869
You already know bigger trades get worse prices.

00:00:34.869 --> 00:00:37.147
This lesson is where that stops being a

00:00:37.147 --> 00:00:39.823
feeling and becomes a number you can calculate

00:00:39.823 --> 00:00:42.499
yourself, before you ever sign the transaction.

00:00:43.799 --> 00:00:44.654
The one rule.

00:00:45.954 --> 00:00:49.140
A constant-product A.M.M. holds two tokens in

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:51.449
a pool, no order book at all.

00:00:51.449 --> 00:00:54.706
It keeps exactly one rule: reserves of token

00:00:54.706 --> 00:00:57.829
A, times reserves of token B, equals k.

00:00:57.829 --> 00:00:59.678
And k has to stay constant,

00:00:59.678 --> 00:01:02.619
ignoring fees, after every single trade.

00:01:02.619 --> 00:01:05.863
Pool price is just the ratio of those two

00:01:05.863 --> 00:01:10.730
reserves: a hundred E.T.H. and three hundred thousand U.S.D.C.

00:01:10.730 --> 00:01:14.979
means one E.T.H. is worth about three thousand U.S.D.C.

00:01:16.279 --> 00:01:17.918
Here's that rule playing out

00:01:17.918 --> 00:01:19.744
as an actual trade happens.

00:01:19.744 --> 00:01:22.129
You swap ETH into the pool.

00:01:22.129 --> 00:01:25.792
The pool has to remove enough USDC to keep

00:01:25.792 --> 00:01:29.159
x times y equal to k, no exceptions.

00:01:29.159 --> 00:01:31.342
That shift moves the price against the

00:01:31.342 --> 00:01:33.525
next trader; the further you moved the

00:01:33.525 --> 00:01:35.784
pool, the bigger that price impact.

00:01:35.784 --> 00:01:38.050
Arbitrageurs then trade the pool back

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:40.259
in line with every other market.

00:01:40.259 --> 00:01:44.214
And that rebalancing is exactly what changes what an

00:01:44.214 --> 00:01:47.349
LP holds, which Lesson 2.4 covers in full.

00:01:48.649 --> 00:01:49.739
Quick check.

00:01:49.739 --> 00:01:53.243
A pool holds fifty E.T.H. and one hundred fifty

00:01:53.243 --> 00:01:57.184
thousand U.S.D.C. What's k, and what's the spot price?

00:02:01.604 --> 00:02:05.174
The answer: k is seven point five million, and

00:02:05.174 --> 00:02:08.819
spot price is three thousand U.S.D.C. per E.T.H.

00:02:10.120 --> 00:02:11.065
A worked swap.

00:02:12.365 --> 00:02:16.142
Start with a pool holding one hundred E.T.H. and

00:02:16.142 --> 00:02:20.536
three hundred thousand U.S.D.C., so k is thirty million,

00:02:20.536 --> 00:02:24.621
and spot price is three thousand U.S.D.C. per E.T.H.

00:02:24.621 --> 00:02:27.465
Swap in ten E.T.H., fees ignored.

00:02:27.465 --> 00:02:29.487
New E.T.H. side: one hundred

00:02:29.487 --> 00:02:31.600
plus ten, one hundred ten.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.826
New U.S.D.C. side: thirty million divided by

00:02:34.826 --> 00:02:37.908
one hundred ten, about two hundred seventy

00:02:37.908 --> 00:02:41.075
two thousand seven hundred twenty seven.

00:02:41.075 --> 00:02:44.851
You receive the difference: three hundred thousand minus

00:02:44.851 --> 00:02:48.428
that, about twenty seven thousand two hundred seventy

00:02:48.428 --> 00:02:52.138
three U.S.D.C. Your effective price: about two thousand

00:02:52.138 --> 00:02:55.583
seven hundred twenty seven per E.T.H., roughly nine

00:02:55.583 --> 00:02:57.835
point one percent worse than spot.

00:02:59.135 --> 00:03:01.670
Now try the same pool at three sizes.

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:07.395
1 ETH: new ETH side 101, you'd receive about

00:03:07.395 --> 00:03:13.165
2,970 USDC, only around 1% worse than spot.

00:03:13.165 --> 00:03:15.553
10 ETH, the trade you just

00:03:15.553 --> 00:03:18.772
worked through, about 9.1% worse.

00:03:18.772 --> 00:03:24.050
And 50 ETH: new ETH side 150, you'd receive

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:30.107
about 100,000 USDC, roughly 33% worse than spot.

00:03:30.107 --> 00:03:31.681
Ten times the size cost

00:03:31.681 --> 00:03:33.884
about nine times more impact.

00:03:33.884 --> 00:03:36.939
Five times that again cost more than triple again.

00:03:36.939 --> 00:03:39.978
Size relative to the pool's depth is what matters,

00:03:39.978 --> 00:03:42.182
not the size of the trade in dollars.

00:03:43.482 --> 00:03:44.572
Quick check.

00:03:44.572 --> 00:03:46.845
Why does a ten E.T.H. swap get

00:03:46.845 --> 00:03:49.264
a worse average price than a one

00:03:49.264 --> 00:03:51.537
E.T.H. swap, in that same pool?

00:03:55.957 --> 00:03:58.192
The answer: each unit you add moves

00:03:58.192 --> 00:04:00.799
the reserve ratio further, so later units

00:04:00.799 --> 00:04:03.272
in the same trade are priced worse.

00:04:03.272 --> 00:04:06.728
Price impact grows with trade size relative to reserves.

00:04:08.028 --> 00:04:11.093
Size relative to pool depth is what matters.

00:04:11.093 --> 00:04:13.098
Not the size of the trade in dollars.

00:04:14.398 --> 00:04:16.123
Last check for this section.

00:04:16.123 --> 00:04:18.559
Who actually moves the pool price back

00:04:18.559 --> 00:04:20.683
in line after your trade, and why

00:04:20.683 --> 00:04:22.307
does that matter to L.P.s?

00:04:26.727 --> 00:04:28.952
The answer: arbitrageurs do.

00:04:28.952 --> 00:04:32.200
Their trades rebalance what the L.P. holds, which

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:35.708
is exactly where impermanent loss comes from, covered

00:04:35.708 --> 00:04:37.722
fully in Lesson two point four.

00:04:39.022 --> 00:04:40.377
Where this trips people up.

00:04:41.677 --> 00:04:43.507
Here's how people get this wrong

00:04:43.507 --> 00:04:45.582
even once they know the formula.

00:04:45.582 --> 00:04:48.444
Assuming price impact scales in proportion to

00:04:48.444 --> 00:04:50.746
size, it doesn't, it gets worse than

00:04:50.746 --> 00:04:53.037
proportionally, as you just saw.

00:04:53.037 --> 00:04:55.376
Reading the pool's spot price and assuming

00:04:55.376 --> 00:04:57.715
that's what they'll receive, it's only the

00:04:57.715 --> 00:04:59.592
starting point, not the fill.

00:04:59.592 --> 00:05:01.663
Ignoring that fees also sit in the

00:05:01.663 --> 00:05:04.212
pool and shift the real math slightly.

00:05:04.212 --> 00:05:07.946
And skipping the aggregator comparison because the math felt

00:05:07.946 --> 00:05:10.883
done once k was calculated, when a split across

00:05:10.883 --> 00:05:14.617
pools can still beat a single constant-product pool outright.

00:05:15.917 --> 00:05:16.942
Before any swap.

00:05:18.242 --> 00:05:19.590
Run this before any swap

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:21.077
on one of these pools.

00:05:21.077 --> 00:05:23.718
Check the price impact the interface shows; if

00:05:23.718 --> 00:05:26.097
it isn't shown, work it out yourself.

00:05:26.097 --> 00:05:28.197
Compare with an aggregator quote.

00:05:28.197 --> 00:05:31.296
Set your slippage tolerance on purpose, too tight

00:05:31.296 --> 00:05:33.651
and the swap fails, too loose and you

00:05:33.651 --> 00:05:36.007
risk a bad fill or a sandwich attack,

00:05:36.007 --> 00:05:38.972
which lesson two point five covers in full.

00:05:38.972 --> 00:05:41.747
Split large trades, or use deeper pools.

00:05:41.747 --> 00:05:44.151
And run the before-signing checklist:

00:05:44.151 --> 00:05:46.682
chain, contract, token, amount, spender.

00:05:47.982 --> 00:05:50.197
Your turn, 5 to 10 minutes.

00:05:50.197 --> 00:05:52.172
Pick any constant-product pool you

00:05:52.172 --> 00:05:53.882
can see the reserves for.

00:05:53.882 --> 00:05:56.148
Calculate k, and work out what a trade

00:05:56.148 --> 00:05:58.472
of a size you'd actually make would net

00:05:58.472 --> 00:06:00.912
you, before you ever open the swap screen.

00:06:02.212 --> 00:06:05.686
To recap: a constant-product pool keeps reserves

00:06:05.686 --> 00:06:08.168
of token A times reserves of token

00:06:08.168 --> 00:06:09.957
B equal to k, always.

00:06:09.957 --> 00:06:12.302
Price impact grows with your trade size

00:06:12.302 --> 00:06:14.529
relative to the pool's depth, not the

00:06:14.529 --> 00:06:16.529
size of the trade in dollars.

00:06:16.529 --> 00:06:20.743
And arbitrageurs rebalance the pool after every trade, which

00:06:20.743 --> 00:06:24.404
is exactly where impermanent loss for LPs comes from.

00:06:25.705 --> 00:06:28.005
That's AMM mathematics.

00:06:28.005 --> 00:06:31.407
Next up, Lesson 2.3: providing liquidity, and

00:06:31.407 --> 00:06:34.810
estimating what an LP position actually earns.
